// The camera's numbers. Every field not written here takes the engine's default (the numbers the
// camera always had); the only change is the fit (R4): locked on to a point high on a big body (the
// colossus's horn), the view turns up from the player as far as it takes to keep the point in frame,
// and never so far that the player's feet leave it. A target the frame already holds (any body of
// a person's height) is framed exactly as without it.
// The picture: a bloom, so the camera draws in HDR and light brighter than its threshold glows. The
// threshold is well above white, so nothing lit by the sun glows, only effects that say an
// `intensity` (the hit's sparks); the tone mapping stays the camera's own.
// The first person (F1b): the sandbox plays through the orbit camera (`mode` stays third person);
// `camera_mode mode=first` (or `mode: FirstPerson` here) looks through the body's eye instead, with the
// engine's defaults for the ease on steps and the landing dip, and draws the view model `arms`
// (`anim/arms.view_model.ron`) for any body whose moveset names none.
{
    "default": (
        framing: (fit: Some(())),
        picture: (bloom: Some((intensity: 0.3, threshold: 1.5, composite: Additive))),
        first_person: (view_model: Some("arms")),
    ),
}
